WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.773 --> 00:00:02.690
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Hi.

00:00:02.690 --> 00:00:05.150
I'm Priyanka Vergadia,
a developer advocate

00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:06.050
for Google Cloud.

00:00:06.050 --> 00:00:08.240
And in this video,
I'll show you how

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:11.630
to create an e-commerce
website with Google Cloud.

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:14.840
There are multiple ways to
create a retail website.

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:17.810
In this video, I'll walk you
through one such approach

00:00:17.810 --> 00:00:20.150
called headless commerce.

00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:22.550
Headless commerce is
where you decouple

00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:26.960
the frontend, your mobile app,
your CMS, from your backend

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:28.670
or your commerce layer.

00:00:28.670 --> 00:00:30.770
This approach
provides flexibility

00:00:30.770 --> 00:00:33.470
to your marketing
teams to quickly alter

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:36.770
frontend elements to run
campaigns and promotions

00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:39.620
and create the most
advanced customer experience

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:41.540
for any frontend device.

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:43.250
By the end of this
video, you will

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:46.580
understand the basics of how
to build a headless commerce

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:47.670
application.

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:49.940
You will see a frontend
experience deployed

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:53.240
on Cloud Run in Google
Cloud, and integrate it

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:56.990
with commerce backend from
Google Cloud Marketplace

00:00:56.990 --> 00:00:59.510
offering partner,
Commerce Tools.

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:00.080
All right.

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:02.670
Let's get started.

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:03.620
First things first.

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:06.980
If you logged into the
console for the first time,

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:09.030
create a project.

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:12.230
A project is required
to use Google Cloud

00:01:12.230 --> 00:01:16.370
and forms the basis of
creating, enabling, and using

00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:18.650
all the Google Cloud services.

00:01:18.650 --> 00:01:22.610
I'm going to be using this
one for our demo today.

00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:25.340
On the left navigation,
select Billing

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:27.890
and link your billing account.

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:30.570
Go to billing any
time for an accurate,

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:34.130
up to date picture of
your Cloud spending.

00:01:34.130 --> 00:01:37.730
You can view all products
and learn more about them,

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:41.330
read documentation,
work on a quick start,

00:01:41.330 --> 00:01:45.560
and even navigate to
individual products.

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:48.590
If you prefer to use the
command line interface,

00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:52.220
from the top right corner,
you can activate Cloud Shell,

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:54.980
which is an online
terminal preloaded

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:57.980
with utilities such as
the GCloud, Command Line

00:01:57.980 --> 00:02:01.460
Tool, QCTL, and more.

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:04.940
Now that you know how to
navigate through the console,

00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:08.330
let's look into the
headless commerce project.

00:02:08.330 --> 00:02:10.310
Here you have the
Cloud Run instance

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:12.140
for our e-commerce frontend.

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:14.030
It is built on
[INAUDIBLE] storefront

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:17.480
and deployed in a
serverless container.

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:21.140
If you are wondering why we
are using Cloud Run here,

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:24.840
it's because it allows you to
deploy serverless containers.

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:26.540
You don't need to
worry about scaling

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:30.230
up or down as traffic
patterns change.

00:02:30.230 --> 00:02:32.300
Once the service
is deployed, you

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:36.960
can see it in the Cloud
Run Service Details page.

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:41.500
And here is what the
service looks like.

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:45.470
In order for this to work, we
need to plug into a backend.

00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:48.310
So let's talk about
the backend now.

00:02:48.310 --> 00:02:50.770
We could just create
a backend ourselves.

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:52.870
But that would
require us to create

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:57.090
all the microservices for
products, orders, cards, et

00:02:57.090 --> 00:02:57.710
cetera.

00:02:57.710 --> 00:03:00.820
And we have an
example for that here.

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:04.690
Creating your own backend
can be a lot of work

00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:09.190
and can be time consuming, time
which your development team can

00:03:09.190 --> 00:03:12.850
spend elsewhere, which
is why, in this solution,

00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:16.540
we are using a headless
approach, a faster way

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:20.140
to connect with
pre-built backend APIs.

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:22.450
The benefit of this
approach is that you

00:03:22.450 --> 00:03:26.800
can update the frontend without
having to touch the backend.

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:29.710
This separation
also makes it easier

00:03:29.710 --> 00:03:32.800
to update the frontend
while maintaining compliance

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:35.590
with security and other
standards for the backend.

00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:38.770
It also helps with maintaining
consistent performance

00:03:38.770 --> 00:03:41.110
for the end-to-end
client experience

00:03:41.110 --> 00:03:44.620
as you apply updates
to your frontend.

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:45.130
All right.

00:03:45.130 --> 00:03:48.070
So how do we actually do this?

00:03:48.070 --> 00:03:50.050
We have Google
Cloud Marketplace,

00:03:50.050 --> 00:03:53.590
which offers quick deploy
software solutions.

00:03:53.590 --> 00:03:57.040
The partners such as
Commerce Tools on Marketplace

00:03:57.040 --> 00:03:59.740
allows you to create
this integration

00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:02.110
right inside the console.

00:04:02.110 --> 00:04:04.870
After setting up a trial
account on Commerce Tools

00:04:04.870 --> 00:04:07.810
and logging in, I
created an API client

00:04:07.810 --> 00:04:12.220
to connect the frontend with the
Commerce Tools back end, which

00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:20.230
offers products, categories,
customers, orders,

00:04:20.230 --> 00:04:22.990
and other services.

00:04:22.990 --> 00:04:26.620
After both the frontend
and backend are set up,

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:31.460
we can deploy the website
and see that it works.

00:04:31.460 --> 00:04:34.220
And as we saw at the
beginning of this tutorial,

00:04:34.220 --> 00:04:36.730
the storefront on
Cloud Run can be

00:04:36.730 --> 00:04:40.840
accessed at the deployed URL.

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:43.960
You can also see the logs
and metrics in the Cloud Run

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:48.280
deployed service with
request count, latency,

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:53.940
billable instance time, and
other utilization metrics.

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:57.870
You just learned how to build
a headless commerce application

00:04:57.870 --> 00:04:59.760
with frontend
experience deployed

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:04.410
on Cloud Run and backend APIs on
Commerce Tools in Google Cloud

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:05.500
Marketplace.

00:05:05.500 --> 00:05:06.700
Thanks for watching.

00:05:06.700 --> 00:05:09.540
I encourage you to sign
up for a free trial

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:11.940
and follow this
tutorial yourself.

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:14.690
[MUSIC PLAYING]

